--TEST--
Core\Jwt::verifyIssued answers the type its call site wrote, decoding a verified payload as Core\Json::decodeAs decodes a document, and the issuer's key reaches it as a set or on its own — `rule:security/protocol-roster`, `rule:security/verification-does-not-launder`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// Agreement, over the two spellings of a key and the two things a written type
// may be. One question — "what did the claims decode into?" — is asked with the
// issuer's key held directly and with the same key inside the set the issuer
// publishes, and with an inline shape and a deriving class. A member that read
// a set as anything but a lookup, or that grew a decoder of its own beside
// `Core\Json`'s, would still print plausibly against any one of these alone.
#[Core\Json\Derive]
final class Session {
    public tainted string $iss;
    public tainted string $aud;
    public tainted string $sub;
    public int $iat;
    public int $exp;

    public function constructor(
        tainted string $iss,
        tainted string $aud,
        tainted string $sub,
        int $iat,
        int $exp
    ) {
        $this->iss = $iss;
        $this->aud = $aud;
        $this->sub = $sub;
        $this->iat = $iat;
        $this->exp = $exp;
    }
}

Core\Crypto\KeyPair $issuer = Core\Crypto::generateKeyPair(Core\Crypto\KeyKind::Ed25519);

string $token = Core\Jwt::signObject(
    Core\Arr::shapeAs<{iss: string, aud: string, sub: string}>([
        "iss" => "https://issuer.example",
        "aud" => "novis-app",
        "sub" => "ada",
    ]),
    15m,
    $issuer
);

var $direct = Core\Jwt::verifyIssued<
    tainted {iss: string, aud: string, sub: string, iat: int, exp: int}
>($token, $issuer->publicKey(), "https://issuer.example", "novis-app");
echo $direct->sub, " of ", $direct->iss, " for ", $direct->exp - $direct->iat, "s", "\n";

// The same key, reached the way a program that fetched a JWKS reaches it. A
// document of one key is the shape a token carrying no `kid` is answered by.
string $jwk = Core\Encoding::decodeText(
    $issuer->publicKey()->write(Core\Crypto\KeyFormat::Jwk),
    Core\Charset::Utf8
);
Core\Jwt\KeySet $published = Core\Jwt\KeySet::read("{\"keys\":[" . $jwk . "]}");

Session $viaSet = Core\Jwt::verifyIssued<Session>(
    $token,
    $published,
    "https://issuer.example",
    "novis-app"
);
echo $viaSet->sub, " of ", $viaSet->iss, " for ", $viaSet->exp - $viaSet->iat, "s", "\n";

// The boundary on the written type itself: a token carries one payload object,
// so a list names a document no token has. It is a bug in the program rather
// than a verdict on the token, and it is answered before the token is read.
try {
    var $listed = Core\Jwt::verifyIssued<array<tainted {sub: string}>>(
        $token,
        $issuer->publicKey(),
        "https://issuer.example",
        "novis-app"
    );
    echo "a list decoded", "\n";
} catch (LogicError $bug) {
    echo $bug->message, "\n";
}
--EXPECT--
ada of https://issuer.example for 900s
ada of https://issuer.example for 900s
Core\Jwt::verifyIssued(): a token carries one payload object, so `<array<…>>` names a document no token has. Write the type one token's claims decode into.
